===Etymology 2===
{{etymid|ja|nekoma}}
{{ja-kanjitab|yomi=k|ねこま|alt=猫ま}}

According to the {{m|ja||{{w|ja|和名類聚抄}}|tr={{w|Wamyō Ruijushō}}}}, 931–938, {{ja-r|猫|linkto=猫#Japanese:_neko|ねこ}} is short for this word.
: {{RQ:Wamyō Ruijushō|7|56}}
: {{ux|ja|猫: 野王案、猫、音苗、'''禰古麻'''、下總本有和名二字興河海抄引此合、本草和名同訓、或省云禰古、新撰字鏡、狸、禰古、按狸一名猫、見本草和名、似虎而小、熊捕鼠為糧|t=Cat. Recorded as a long jewel. The Chinese reading is myō; [in Japanese it is read as] ''nekoma''. {{sub|(please add a translation of the rest of this usage example) {{attn|ja|please do this}}''}} }}

One theory describes the first [[mora]] <ne> as [[onomatopoeia]] for the sound a cat makes (cf. {{ja-r|にゃ}}; compare {{noncog|en|[[mew]], [[meow]]}}). The last two morae <ko<sub>1</sub>ma> might accord with {{ja-r|熊|くま|bear}} if it were from {{m+|jpx-pro|*koma|pos=class 2.3 <LL>}}, in the sense of "four-legged animal". The Heian Kyoto accent of this word is <LHL>; note that in compound words for species names, the pitch pattern may be simplified to <-HL> when the final element is a 2-mora noun (e.g. {{m|ja|青海苔|tr=aonori|t=green laver|pos=< àwònórì < *àwò-nòrì}}, and the presence or absence of this phenomenon could explain the accent confusion in ''neko''.
{{rfv-etym|ja}}

However, ''nekoma'' is first cited to 918 in the {{m|ja||{{w|lang=ja|本草和名}}|tr=Honzō Wamyō|pos=the oldest surviving dictionary of medicine in Japan}},<ref name="KDJ2o-nekoma"/> while [[猫#Japanese:_neko|''neko'']] is first attested in 794.
: {{RQ:Honzō Wamyō}}
: {{ux|ja|家狸、一名猫、和名'''禰古末'''|t=A house raccoon; also called a cat; the Japanese name is ''nekoma''.}}

